AWS-013 — Workflow Versioning

Document ID: AWS-013
Title: Workflow Versioning
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Workflow Versioning Model (CWVM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Workflow Versioning Model establishes the principles, structures, compatibility rules, lineage mechanisms, and governance required to manage the evolution of Workflow Definitions, Workflow Interfaces, Workflow Programs, and Workflow Compositions while preserving execution reproducibility, interoperability, and historical continuity.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Workflow version architecture
Version identifiers
Semantic versioning
Compatibility
Lineage
Migration
Deprecation
Version governance

This specification applies to every Workflow Definition and related execution artifact.

3. Canonical Definition

Workflow Versioning is the governed management of successive revisions of Workflow Definitions and related workflow artifacts while preserving identity, compatibility, traceability, and execution reproducibility.

Versioning governs workflow evolution.

It SHALL NOT modify historical workflow executions.

4. Objectives

The Workflow Versioning Model SHALL:

Preserve workflow identity.
Support safe evolution.
Enable multiple active versions.
Preserve execution reproducibility.
Support migration.
Enable compatibility assessment.
Preserve lineage.
Maintain governance.
5. Versioning Principles

Every workflow version SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Immutable Releases

Published workflow versions SHALL remain immutable.

Identity Preservation

All workflow versions SHALL preserve the canonical Workflow ID.

Explicit Versioning

Every Workflow Definition SHALL declare an explicit version identifier.

Historical Reproducibility

Historical Workflow Instances SHALL always reference the exact version executed.

Compatibility Transparency

Compatibility relationships SHALL be explicitly declared.

Governed Evolution

Workflow versions SHALL evolve only through governed lifecycle transitions.

6. Version Structure

Every workflow version SHALL define:

Property	Description
Workflow ID	Stable canonical identity
Version	Semantic version
Publication Date	Official release date
Lifecycle Status	Draft, Active, Deprecated, etc.
Compatibility Profile	Supported versions
Lineage Reference	Previous version
Governance Approval	Approval record
7. Semantic Versioning

Atlas adopts canonical semantic versioning.

Version Component	Meaning
Major	Breaking execution change
Minor	Backward-compatible enhancement
Patch	Corrections without semantic change

Example:

2.4.7
│ │ │
│ │ └── Patch
│ └──── Minor
└────── Major

Major revisions SHALL require compatibility assessment.

8. Version Compatibility

Atlas defines the following compatibility states.

Compatibility	Description
Fully Compatible	No migration required
Backward Compatible	New version accepts older integrations
Forward Compatible	Older integrations operate with newer version
Conditionally Compatible	Migration constraints apply
Incompatible	Explicit migration required

Compatibility SHALL be explicitly documented.

9. Version Lineage

Workflow versions SHALL define lineage.

Example:

Workflow v1.0
      │
      ▼
Workflow v1.1
      │
      ▼
Workflow v2.0
      │
      ▼
Workflow v2.1

Lineage SHALL remain permanently reconstructable.

10. Migration

Migration SHALL define:

Source version
Target version
Migration authority
Required transformations
Compatibility assessment
Validation requirements
Rollback strategy

Migration SHALL NOT alter historical Workflow Instances.

11. Coexistence

Atlas permits simultaneous execution of multiple workflow versions.

Example:

Workflow v2.1
      │
      ├── Project A
      ├── Project B
      │
Workflow v3.0
      │
      ├── Project C
      └── Project D

Each Workflow Instance SHALL reference its originating version.

12. Deprecation

Every deprecated workflow SHALL define:

Deprecation rationale
Replacement version
Effective date
Migration guidance
Retirement schedule
Governance approval

Deprecated workflows MAY continue executing existing Workflow Instances.

13. Version Governance

Every workflow version SHALL define:

Version owner
Approval authority
Review authority
Applicable policies
Lifecycle status
Audit references

Governance SHALL remain attached throughout version evolution.

14. Version Quality

Every workflow version SHALL satisfy:

Correctness
Traceability
Compatibility
Reproducibility
Explainability
Auditability
Governance compliance
Historical integrity

Quality SHALL be independently verifiable.

15. Version Events

Versioning MAY generate Canonical Events.

Examples include:

Workflow Version Created
Workflow Version Approved
Workflow Version Published
Workflow Deprecated
Workflow Retired
Workflow Migration Completed

Events SHALL conform to ADS-006 — Canonical Event (CEV).

16. Conformance

A Workflow Versioning implementation conforms if it:

Implements semantic versioning.
Preserves immutable releases.
Maintains workflow lineage.
Supports coexistence.
Supports governed migration.
Preserves historical reproducibility.
Maintains version traceability.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-assisted compatibility analysis.
Automated migration planning.
Predictive deprecation analysis.
Federated workflow version synchronization.
Machine-verifiable compatibility proofs.
Adaptive version recommendation.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical versioning semantics.

